meta { title = "Byte streams", description = "Copy binary data with a reusable ByteBuffer and generic readers and writers.", order = 8, group = "Library reference" }

Use ByteReader and ByteWriter from std/stream when a function needs to move bytes between endpoints. A File and a TcpStream implement both traits. A split TCP reader or writer implements its corresponding trait. A BufferedReader implements ByteReader and a BufferedWriter implements ByteWriter.

h2 { "Copy a file with one reusable buffer" }

Run this example in an empty directory. It writes a greeting to source.bin and makes two copies. The same pump works with raw files and with buffered files. finish flushes the buffered output and reports any final write error.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """from "std/stream" import ByteReader, ByteWriter, StreamError, readInto, writeAll
from "std/fs" import open, close, OpenMode, FsError, writeText, bufferedReader, bufferedWriter, finish
from "std/buffer" import ByteBuffer, byteBuffer, BufferError

fn pump[R: ByteReader, W: ByteWriter](source: R, target: W, mut chunk: ByteBuffer) -> u64 ! StreamError:
  total: u64 = 0
  while true:
    count = try readInto(source, chunk, 65536)
    if count == 0:
      break
    try writeAll(target, chunk)
    total = total + count
  return total

fn main() -> unit ! (FsError | StreamError | BufferError):
  seed = try open("source.bin", OpenMode.Create)
  try writeText(seed, "Hello from Kalandra")
  try close(seed)
  chunk = try byteBuffer(65536)
  source = try open("source.bin", OpenMode.Read)
  target = try open("target.bin", OpenMode.Create)
  total = try pump(source, target, chunk)
  try close(target)
  try close(source)
  reader = try bufferedReader(try open("target.bin", OpenMode.Read), 8192)
  writer = try bufferedWriter(try open("buffered.bin", OpenMode.Create), 4096)
  again = try pump(reader, writer, chunk)
  try finish(writer)
  try close(reader)
  return ()
""" }

h2 { "readInto" }

code { "readInto[S: ByteReader](stream: S, mut buffer: ByteBuffer, maxBytes: u64) -> u64 ! StreamError" }

Replace the initialized contents with up to maxBytes bytes. A positive request returning zero means EOF. A short positive read delivers data and the next read may deliver more. A zero-byte request cannot establish EOF.

h2 { "readAppend" }

code { "readAppend[S: ByteReader](stream: S, mut buffer: ByteBuffer, maxBytes: u64) -> u64 ! StreamError" }

Append up to maxBytes bytes, returning the number added. The earlier prefix stays available on EOF or error.

h2 { "write" }

code { "write[S: ByteWriter](stream: S, buffer: ByteBuffer) -> u64 ! StreamError" }

Write from the initialized prefix and return the number accepted. A short write is possible. To deliver the whole buffer, use writeAll.

h2 { "writeAll" }

code { "writeAll[S: ByteWriter](stream: S, buffer: ByteBuffer) -> unit ! StreamError" }

Write the entire initialized prefix, suspending as space becomes available. On error some bytes may already have been delivered. StreamError.BrokenPipe carries the native code for a closed peer. StreamError.System carries the OS code and a diagnostic message. StreamError.InvalidArgument reports an incompatible operation. TCP endpoints with a time budget report StreamError.TimedOut when it expires.

h2 { "close" }

code { "close[S: PipeCloser](own stream: S) -> unit ! StreamError" }

Consume a pipe or TCP endpoint. Closing the last pipe writer or the TCP writer half delivers EOF after pending bytes have been read. Owned endpoints also close when their scope exits. Use std/fs.close for files and std/fs.finish to flush and close a BufferedWriter.

